    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, let us first find out the prime factors of Alice's number, which is 24.\n\n**Step 1:** Finding the prime factors of 24\n24 is given to be the number Alice talked about. Now, let us first look at its prime decomposition.\n\n![image](https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/265269ec-faa9-4c2c-bb01-64a223751ddb_1616450332.5883688.png)\n\n**Step 2:** Calculating Bob's number\nGiven that Bob needs every prime factor taken into consideration by him, we need to factorize 24 again and then include the additional prime factors too. The prime factorization of 24 upwards would look like:\n\n![image](https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/560b5d7c-d789-4cc7-a280-ae849cb1e900_1616450351.6359443.png)\n\nNow, let us consider that to make a smallest possible number, all of Bob's prime factors should also exist as prime factors in Alice's number and all other prime factors in Bob's number should be exclusive to Bob's number.\n\nThus, the smallest possible number for Bob can only use the prime factors that Alice's number already uses, none of them may be used for either numbers and added parts of the number should be different from each other. \n\nTherefore, the smallest possible number that Bob could have will be 2.\n\nHowever, by definition, the smallest positive integer in this context refers to typical use of $1$. By convention, if no prime factors exist, $1$ should be included, enabling the smallest equally logical solution.\n\nTherefore, the prime factors that Bob's number should use must be the same ones contemporaryly used for Alice’s number, with respect to_prime factors of 24, which is $\\{2,3\\}$, making 21 the smallest possible number that Bob could have if the conventional mindset is adopted, here this means that $ \\boxed{11} $ could have been the smallest possible number.",
